Ques 2. Circular Queue Using Array: 
A Circular Queue is a linear data structure that follows the FIFO (First In, First Out) principle.
In a circular queue, the last position of the array is connected back to the first position. This allows us to reuse the empty spaces created after deleting elements.

Operations:
ENQUEUE(x) → Inserts an element into the queue.
DEQUEUE() → Removes an element from the front.
FRONT() → Returns the front element without removing it.
DISPLAY() → Displays all elements of the queue.

#include <stdio.h>
#define MAX 5
int queue[MAX];
int front = -1;
int rear = -1;

// ENQUEUE operation
void ENQUEUE(int x)
{
    // Check if queue is full
    if ((rear + 1) % MAX == front)
    {
        printf("Queue Overflow! Queue is full.\n");
        return;
    }
    // First element
    if (front == -1)
    {
        front = 0;
        rear = 0;
    }
    else
    {
        rear = (rear + 1) % MAX;
    }

    queue[rear] = x;

    printf("%d inserted into queue.\n", x);
}

// DEQUEUE operation
void DEQUEUE()
{
    if (front == -1)
    {
        printf("Queue Underflow! Queue is empty.\n");
        return;
    }
    printf("%d deleted from queue.\n", queue[front]);

    // If only one element is present
    if (front == rear)
    {
        front = -1;
        rear = -1;
    }
    else
    {
        front = (front + 1) % MAX;
    }
}

// FRONT operation
void FRONT()
{
    if (front == -1)
    {
        printf("Queue is empty.\n");
        return;
    }
    printf("Front element = %d\n", queue[front]);
}

// DISPLAY operation
void DISPLAY()
{
    if (front == -1)
    {
        printf("Queue is empty.\n");
        return;
    }

    printf("Queue elements: ");
    int i = front;
    while (1)
    {
        printf("%d ", queue[i]);

        if (i == rear)
            break;

        i = (i + 1) % MAX;
    }

    printf("\n");
}

int main()
{
    ENQUEUE(10);
    ENQUEUE(20);
    ENQUEUE(30);
    ENQUEUE(40);

    DISPLAY();

    FRONT();

    DEQUEUE();
    DEQUEUE();

    DISPLAY();

    ENQUEUE(50);
    ENQUEUE(60);

    DISPLAY();

    return 0;
}

How Circular Queue Works:
Suppose the size is 5:
Index:   0    1    2    3    4
        [10] [20] [30] [40] [50]
After deleting 10 and 20, positions 0 and 1 become empty.
In a normal queue, these spaces might be wasted. But in a circular queue, rear can move back to these positions: 
Index:   0    1    2    3    4
        [60] [70] [30] [40] [50]
         ↑              ↑
       Rear            Front
The movement is done using: rear = (rear + 1) % MAX;

Empty Queue: The queue is empty when:
front == -1
Therefore: Queue Underflow! Queue is empty.

Full Queue: The queue is full when:
(rear + 1) % MAX == front
Therefore: Queue Overflow! Queue is full.

Important Case: When there is only one element:
front == rear
After DEQUEUE(), both are reset: 
front = -1;
rear = -1;

Additional Task: Circular Queue vs Linear Queue- A linear queue and a circular queue both follow the FIFO (First In, First Out) principle, but they differ in how they use the available array space.

1. Why does a circular queue provide better utilization of memory?
In a linear queue, when elements are deleted from the front, the empty positions at the beginning cannot normally be reused once REAR has reached the last index.
A circular queue solves this problem by connecting the last index back to the first index. Therefore, when space becomes available at the beginning, new elements can be inserted there.
Linear Queue:
[  ] [  ] [30] [40] [50]
 ↑     ↑             ↑
unused positions    REAR
In a circular queue, REAR can wrap around:
[60] [70] [30] [40] [50]
 ↑         ↑
REAR      FRONT

2. Time Complexity of ENQUEUE and DEQUEUE? 
Operation: ENQUEUE
Linear Queue: O(1)
Circular Queue: O(1)

Operation: DEQUEUE
Linear Queue: O(1)
Circular Queue: O(1)

3. Space Complexity?
Both queues use an array of fixed size MAX.
Therefore:
Linear Queue: O(n)
Circular Queue: O(n)
where n is the capacity of the queue.
#define MAX 5 //then the queue requires space for 5 elements.

4. Problem When REAR Reaches the Last Index in a Linear Queue? 
This is called the false overflow problem.
Suppose the queue has a capacity of 5:
[  ] [  ] [30] [40] [50]
     FRONT       REAR
The first two positions are empty because elements were previously deleted.
Queue Overflow
even though there are actually two unused positions at the beginning. This is known as false overflow.
A circular queue avoids this problem by allowing REAR to wrap around to the beginning:
[60] [70] [30] [40] [50]
 ↑
REAR
